14:50:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Vitamin B12 is water-soluble, but “water-soluble” doesn’t mean the body *can’t* store it. It mainly describes how the vitamin dissolves and travels in body fluids. B12 is unusual among water-soluble vitamins because the body has specialized systems to absorb, transport, recycle, and store it: - **It binds tightly to carrier proteins** in the blood (such as transcobalamin), rather than freely floating around to be filtered into urine. - **The liver actively takes it up and stores it.** Adults commonly have enough liver B12 stored for several years. - **It is recycled through bile.** The liver releases B12 into bile, and much of it is reabsorbed in the intestine rather than lost in stool or urine. This is called enterohepatic circulation. - **Kidneys mostly excrete excess only when binding/uptake capacity is exceeded.** Normally, little B12 is lost in urine. This storage makes biological sense: B12 is needed in tiny amounts but is harder to obtain and absorb than many other vitamins. Absorption requires stomach acid, intrinsic factor, and a specific uptake site in the last part of the small intestine. Keeping a reserve protects against short-term gaps in dietary intake. Other water-soluble vitamins can also be stored to some degree—especially **folate**—but B12 has by far the largest and longest-lasting stores.
